# Pattern: Reference Data Validation

**Dimension:** Accuracy

## The problem
Fields that should map to a known, authoritative reference set (country codes,
product categories, currency codes) can drift — new values appear that don't exist
in the reference list, often from typos, upstream system changes, or manual entry.
These "orphan" values are usually a sign the data no longer accurately reflects
reality.

## When to use it
- Any field with a defined, finite set of valid values maintained outside the
  pipeline (a reference/lookup table, an external standard like ISO country codes)
- Especially useful after a source system change or new data feed onboarding,
  where drift is most likely to first appear

## How it works
1. Identify the authoritative reference set for the field (a lookup table, an
   external standard, or a client-provided list)
2. Compare every value in the field against the reference set
3. Flag any value that doesn't exist in the reference set
4. Decide handling: hard fail (block the pipeline), soft fail (flag and continue),
   or quarantine (route unmatched records to a review table)

## What "good" looks like
- 100% of values match the reference set, or unmatched values are explicitly
  triaged rather than silently passed through
- The reference set itself is version-controlled and dated, so you can tell if
  a failure is due to bad data or a stale reference list

## Known limitations
- Only as good as the reference set — if the reference data itself is outdated,
  this check will produce false failures
- Doesn't catch values that are technically valid but contextually wrong
  (e.g. a real country code that's still the wrong country for this record)